---
// The 5-part tutorial outline, following the EMNLP 2026 proposal. Part 1 sets
// up the agent development loop; Parts 2 to 5 each follow the same arc: failure
// patterns, diagnostics, fixes, and (where the proposal lists them) open
// challenges. Safety is threaded throughout, and Part 5 gathers it with evaluation.

// Column layout for Parts 2 to 5.
const ARC = [
  { key: 'failures', label: 'Failure patterns', cls: 'col-fail' },
  { key: 'diagnostics', label: 'Diagnostics', cls: 'col-diag' },
  { key: 'fixes', label: 'Fixes', cls: 'col-fix' },
];

const parts = [
  {
    n: 'Part 1',
    title: 'The Development Loop of AI Agents',
    minutes: 10,
    lead: 'Cheng Qian',
    summary:
      'An agent is not a single LLM call but a closed loop: it perceives, plans, acts through tools, and observes over many steps while persisting state in memory. We survey the landscape and lay out a build lifecycle that maps onto the rest of the tutorial.',
    columns: [
      {
        label: 'Agent landscape',
        cls: 'col-info',
        items: [
          'Open-source personal assistants (OpenClaw, Hermes Agent)',
          'Coding agents (Devin, Claude Code)',
          'Browser and computer-use agents (OpenAI Operator, ChatGPT Agent, Claude Computer Use)',
          'Deep-research agents (DR Tulu)',
          'Embodied agents (π*0.6)',
        ],
      },
      {
        label: 'Build lifecycle',
        cls: 'col-info',
        items: [
          'Scaffold and tools for multi-turn trajectories (Part 2)',
          'Post-training with SFT and RL (Part 3)',
          'Memory, skills, and test-time adaptation (Part 4)',
          'Evaluation, safety, and open frontiers (Part 5)',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 2',
    title: 'Agent Environments and Infrastructure',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Zoey Sha Li',
    summary:
      'Training an agent means turning what the model writes into calls, tokens, and rewards. We cover the tool-call protocol, how SFT and rewards get a model to call tools at all, and the RL system and environments behind agent training: search APIs, code sandboxes, and a container per software task. Every failure in this part sits at a seam between the model and its harness.',
    failures: [
      'Cold start: the model barely calls its tools, so on-policy RL has little to reinforce',
      "Token misattribution: the environment's tokens end up in the loss",
      'Re-tokenization drift: decoding, parsing, and re-encoding a turn changes the token IDs the trainer sees',
      "Environment errors scored as the policy's: failed environments, empty results that lead to void turns, and slow tools the policy learns to avoid",
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Tool calls per trajectory',
      'Decode the trained tokens and count them against what the inference engine generated',
      'Per-call token counts, and the token IDs when you keep them',
      'Per-tool failure, timeout, and call rates; per-turn checks for void turns',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'An SFT prior whose data covers the behavior (SynPlanResearch-R1), or a reward that pays for each part of a call (ToolRL)',
      'Build the loss mask with the sequence, 1 only for what the model wrote (Search-R1)',
      'Token in, token out: ask the engine for token IDs and tokenize each new message on its own',
      'Rerun or drop trajectories the environment broke, filter trajectories with void turns (SimpleTIR), and keep timeouts out of the reward',
    ],
    challenges: [
      'Environments that are expensive to run: computer use, scientific experiments, and robotics',
      'Building environments at scale, and checking the verifiers that come with them (SWE-Universe)',
      'The harness as a safety surface once agents read untrusted observations and call external tools (GuardAgent)',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 3',
    title: 'Training Dynamics',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Zihan Wang',
    summary:
      'Three failures of the learning signal: collapse, where useful variation disappears; reward hacking, where optimization drifts from the intended objective; and no success signal, where no successful rollout enters training.',
    failures: [
      'Collapse: training gains disappear, success can reinforce a false explanation (RAGEN), and high entropy can hide generic, input-agnostic reasoning (RAGEN-2)',
      'Reward hacking: more proxy reward can lower true quality (Constrained RLHF); agents retrieve answers from git history, move work outside a timed check, and pass hacks to other agents',
      'No success signal: when every rollout in a group fails, centered rewards are zero and nothing is reinforced',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Input dependence: can a reasoning trace identify its own input? (cross-input scoring, RAGEN-2)',
      'Outcome contrast: within-group reward variance, and noise from re-tokenization and training-inference mismatch',
      'Proxy points found with independent evaluation; re-running fresh agents after sealing the answer channel',
      'Reward histograms that tell all-fail groups from all-succeed ones',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Filtering for informative prompt groups (RAGEN-2), exploration control at the update, token, and turn level (DAPO, T²PO), and importance-sampling correction for engine mismatch',
      'Component targets for composite rewards (Constrained RLHF); measure the intended operation and repair the grader that actually runs',
      'Partial and denser rewards (VAGEN, BAGEN), more samples on unresolved prompts (NGU), external information (LUFFY, POPE, NuRL), and dense-to-binary reward curricula',
    ],
    challenges: [
      'Useful learning signal without narrowing task coverage',
      'Separating environment randomness from learning signal',
      'Testing whether reasoning is causally faithful to the input',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 4',
    title: 'Test-Time Scaling, Adaptation, and Recursive Self-Improvement',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Hejie Cui',
    summary:
      'Four places where deployment-time improvement fails: test-time scaling, where more reasoning can lower accuracy; memory and skills, where what the agent keeps goes stale or makes it worse; recursive self-improvement, where the agent edits its own harness; and autoresearch, the same loop pointed at research.',
    failures: [
      'More reasoning can lower accuracy, and agents misjudge how much budget they have left (BAGEN)',
      'Memories go stale without being contradicted (STALE), and self-written skills can do worse than none (SkillsBench)',
      'Long search histories anchor a self-improving agent to its past attempts',
      'Autoresearch runs leave flaws they notice unfixed, overclaim, and hack their own evaluations',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Accuracy against tokens, turns, tool calls, and improvement rounds',
      'Probes of compressed memory beyond the compression ratio (context collapse, ACE)',
      'Hidden-set replay of the final artifact (RSI-Exam), and regression checks on tasks the improver did not predict (AHE)',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Budget-aware stopping and budget trackers (BAGEN, Budget Tracker), and length penalties on steps that do not move the answer (SWAP)',
      'RL-trained memory managers (Memory-R1, MemAgent), context folding, and strategies distilled from successes and failures (ReasoningBank)',
      'Small edits kept only on held-out improvement, evolved on benchmark-disjoint tasks (ModularRSI, SkillOpt)',
      'Keep the whole research record: agent-native research artifacts with the dead ends and the evidence behind each claim',
    ],
    challenges: [
      'Attribution: which behavior did each harness edit actually change?',
      'Predicting what an edit will break as well as what it fixes',
      'Edits that compose without interfering',
      'What a search loop should remember: shared memory helps, long history anchors',
      'Oversight that holds when research agents learn from their reviewers',
      'Verification beyond the benchmark the loop was tuned on',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 5',
    title: 'Evaluation, Safety, and Open Frontiers',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Manling Li',
    summary:
      'Leaderboard scores routinely fail to measure what we think. We show where static benchmarks break, how closed-loop evaluation restores signal, and how red teaming probes agent attack surfaces, including those flagged in Parts 2 to 4. The part closes with open frontiers and the synthesis.',
    failures: [
      'Contamination: test instances leak into training, so the score reflects recall',
      'Saturation: a benchmark stops discriminating as models reach its ceiling (SWE-bench)',
      'Construct invalidity: the benchmark does not isolate the intended ability',
      'Harness variance: the same backbone scores differently across scaffolds and tools',
      'Attack surfaces: prompt injection, reward hacking under safety constraints, memory poisoning',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Black-box tests for test-set contamination',
      'Harness-controlled comparisons of one backbone across scaffolds (Harness-Bench)',
      'The attack surface as a measurable axis under adversarial inputs (Agent Security Bench)',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Dynamic, live, and asynchronous evaluation that resists memorization and restores headroom (ARE/Gaia2, Online-Mind2Web)',
      'Social, embodied, and out-of-sync recovery evaluation (SOTOPIA, SyncMind)',
      'Verifiable safety-policy reasoning as a runtime defense (ShieldAgent)',
    ],
  },
];

// Part 1 supplies its own columns; the rest use the failure/diagnostic/fix arc.
const outline = parts.map((p) => ({
  ...p,
  cols: p.columns ?? ARC.map((c) => ({ label: c.label, cls: c.cls, items: p[c.key] })),
}));
---
<section id="outline" class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 class="section-title">Tutorial Outline</h2>
      <p class="section-dek">Parts 2 to 5 each follow the same arc: failure patterns, diagnostics, fixes, and open challenges.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="parts">
      {outline.map((p) => (
        <article class="part">
          <div class="split">
            <p class="part-meta">
              <span class="label part-n">{p.n}</span>
              <span class="part-time">{p.minutes} min</span>
              <span class="part-lead">{p.lead}</span>
            </p>
            <div>
              <h3 class="part-title">{p.title}</h3>
              {p.summary && <p class="part-summary">{p.summary}</p>}
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="part-cols" style={`--cols: ${p.cols.length}`}>
            {p.cols.map((c) => (
              <div class={`col ${c.cls}`}>
                <h4 class="label">{c.label}</h4>
                <ul>{c.items.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          {p.challenges && (
            <div class="col part-challenges">
              <h4 class="label">Open challenges</h4>
              <ul>{p.challenges.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
            </div>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Each part opens with an ink rule; its head uses the shared meta column,
     and the arc columns run the full measure below it. */
  .part {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-2xl);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .part:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }

  .part-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-top: 0.35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .part-n { color: var(--rubric); }

  .part-time {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .part-lead {
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .part-title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: var(--weight-heading);
    font-stretch: var(--stretch-heading);
    font-size: var(--text-h3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .part-summary {
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .part-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 3), minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .col { --marker: var(--ink-faint); }
  .col-fail { --marker: var(--rubric); }
  .col-diag { --marker: var(--ochre); }
  .col-fix  { --marker: var(--green); }
  .col-info { --marker: var(--brand); }

  .col h4 {
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--marker);
  }

  .col ul {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .col li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* A short hairline marker in the column's color. */
  .col li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.75em;
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--marker);
  }

  .part-challenges {
    --marker: var(--brand);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .part-challenges ul {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .part-meta {
      flex-direction: row;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      column-gap: var(--space-sm);
      padding-top: 0;
    }
    .part-cols { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-lg); }
    .part-challenges ul { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
